WEBVTT

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.240
What got me started on a life of crime, which is what my mother thought my youthful interest

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:11.380
in jazz implied, was a case of the measles when I was fifteen.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.500
In those days, the early 1930s, when you had the measles, they put you in a darkened room

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.610
and you were not allowed to read.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:24.020
And so my mother let me have the old family radio, and I lay there wide awake in the night,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:29.860
picking up those strange sounds in the night: Duke Ellington, Louis Armstrong.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:36.680
Cab Callaway, Earl Hines, Fletcher Henderson, and all I knew was what I heard, and what

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:42.860
I heard gave a thrust to my life which has never left it. I have learned more from them

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:48.260
than I did in any classroom, and their art has given me a faith in creativity and in

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:53.490
life itself that no pulpit has ever offered.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:02:00.000
For millions of readers in the 1950s, 60s, and 70s, Ralph J. Gleason's personal jazz

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.940
were a powerful introduction to America's most vibrant musical scene. A way of

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.860
appreciating a challenging new art form through the affectionate eyes of a true

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:16.870
disciple. Co-founder of the Monterey Jazz Festival. Co-founder of Rolling Stone

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:22.610
magazine. Contributing writer and editor of Downbeat magazine. The world's first

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:27.170
syndicated jazz columnist at the San Francisco Chronicle for over a quarter

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.000
century. Gleason's literary exploration

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.450
of jazz had only one purpose he wanted people to dig the music the thing that a

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.910
modern jazz musician does which you should really keep in mind when you see

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:47.420
him in concerts I see him in jazz clubs is somewhat similar to looking at a poet

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:52.880
standing in the middle of a supermarket and improvising poetry they are called

00:02:52.880 --> 00:03:00.000
upon by the discipline of this art form to go into public places and to spontaneously

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:17.180
It's a game of chance, and you're one of the minor players.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:23.630
Unlike a poet, unlike the writer of a novel, unlike a painter, they have no opportunity

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:30.000
to take this product that they have created and reform it and correct the mistakes that

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.060
or change the way in which they approach it.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.060
What they do is done for all time right then when they do it.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.890
This is a very unique thing about jazz,

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:45.890
and it's one of the things that gives it a particular quality of aliveness

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:49.900
that makes it one of the most interesting and vital of all contemporary art forms.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:55.900
In 1959, Gleason took the unprecedented step of ushering jazz

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:00.000
from smoky late-night clubs to the blue-gray flickerings

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:06.130
early television broadcasting creating producing and hosting jazz casual the

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:12.720
first nationally televised series devoted entirely to jazz on a stage left

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.900
intentionally barren Gleason invited his friends to fill the studio space with

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:23.740
spontaneous innovations of pure jazz and his friends conjured up a special

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:30.000
magic John Coltrane offered his only television

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.040
in North America.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.680
Count Basie visited on his own birthday,

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:41.120
improvising with a special hand-picked quartet.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.020
Artists agreed to appear on Jazz Casual

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.280
who would have otherwise avoided the camera's glare,

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.440
because Gleason allowed them the freedom

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.830
to play what they wanted for as long as they wanted,

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:57.240
mixing the music with insight and celebration.

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:12.000
There are very few people that I consider qualified to judge whether someone is a jazz player or a jazz singer.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.740
Now, you happen to be one of the few people that I feel is fully qualified.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.320
You could cancel off the show for that.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.420
Am I a jazz singer?

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:19.520
No.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:21.100
Am I a jazz singer?

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.470
You are to me, but I've got a very good friend who thinks you aren't.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:29.670
When sunny gets blue, he breathes.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:06:00.000
sigh of sadness like the wind that stirs the tree to buy my way better definition

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:09.290
of a jazz singer could we possibly have than Common McRae.

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:13.710
Earl Father Hines is really the father of jazz piano,

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:16.680
the most important musicians who has ever played this music.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.640
And today, you really can't play jazz piano

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:22.420
without playing a little bit of Earl Father Hines.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.100
Thanks for that wonderful introduction.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:27.000
Well, you know, I mean it, and you deserve it,

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.610
and we all know this.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.880
Of the original 31 episodes of Jazz Casual produced,

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.700
28 still survive, having been locked away in a vault

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:42.980
since their first and only broadcast some three decades ago.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:46.320
Now digitally remastered, these rare treasures

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:51.280
capture the pure essence of the jazz experience.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:54.180
By bringing together these glimpses of the great creative

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:58.110
artists from the earliest right down to the most modern jazz,

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:00.000
I would like to assist in placing

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:04.840
all of them on the broad cultural stage in their proper perspective.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:10.560
This is a dedication to those musicians for all the great moments, the magnificent highs

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.100
of listening.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:17.900
For all I have learned from them, my true teachers, and above all, for their clear instruction

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:21.210
in how to live.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:24.550
Ralph J. Gleason's Jazz Casual Series.

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:30.000
More than two dozen historic masterpiece performances from the titans of modern jazz.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:36.510
Unseen in over 30 years and available for the first time ever on home video.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:48.310
The Ralph J. Gleason Jazz Casual Series, only on Rhino Home Video.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:32.860
Thank you.

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:30.000
as casual. I'm Ralph Gleason and you've been listening to the music

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.850
of the Julian Cannonball Adderley Quintet. Julian Adderley on alto, Nat Adderley on

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:40.620
cornet, Joe Zavanel on piano, Sam Jones on bass, and Louis Hayes on drums. Julian?

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:46.960
Yes, Ralph? The blues have always been an integral part of jazz. Has the blues changed

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:52.430
much over the years? Actually, the basic idea of the blues I don't think has changed very

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:56.800
much. When you go to sit in with some guys and say, "Let's play the blues," it's always

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:00.000
the same routine. But most bands that

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.300
find themselves playing blues frequently, you get sort of hung with playing the same

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:10.380
passages, especially when you play a lot of blues in the same keys, but it's still probably

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:11.580
the most popular form.

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:12.590
Why is this?

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:19.130
Well, because I think that it encompasses, if any particular form encompasses the total

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:25.390
jazz picture, I think the blues does because we get the pathetic feeling, the strong emotions

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:29.400
and so forth with the format of the 12-bar and 8-bar blues.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:29.640
Yeah.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.260
Has the blues changed much in all the additional harmonies that you might put into it in recent years?

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:41.160
Well, I would say that yes, because, like, for instance, the tune that we just played

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:45.880
was one written by our pianist, Joe Zavenou, and it's called Scotch and Water.

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:48.440
And it's got nothing at all to do with drinking.

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:50.220
It's just a play on words.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:54.500
It's because of the bridge that we added to this tune.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:59.500
After each chorus, we do it like an ordinary A-A-B-A type form.

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:00.000
And that's what I'm saying.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:08.710
And the bridge is all one chord, and it's got that Scotch chord feeling, you know, like bagpipes, the dominant interval that goes through, and that's the Scotch feeling, you see?

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:15.280
Do you do any different things in playing of solos on the blues now than, you know, like with back to King Oliver or Louis Armstrong's days?

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:27.470
Well, frankly, you know, as quietly as people think about it, for the most part, people talk about it, the blues, I think, in those days were a little more involved than they are today.

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:30.000
What they called blues might have

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.240
had more things happening.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:37.280
For instance, you might have blues for four choruses, and then all of a sudden, here comes

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:42.520
an interlude of extraordinary changes for a certain period of time.

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:46.890
Now we have been doing the same kind of thing lately in our band, in that the Scotch and

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:48.290
Water is one example.

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:49.990
We added a bridge.

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:55.990
We have a couple more examples of how we have been altering the blues-flavored things.

00:15:55.990 --> 00:16:00.000
Do you write these out, or are these ad lib blues?

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.220
from a general assumption of the same chords and things?

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:03.640
Well, the tunes are written out.

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:08.830
And, of course, the blowing, as in all jazz improvisation, is ad lib.

00:16:09.130 --> 00:16:11.650
When you say the tunes, you mean the melodic lines?

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:14.890
The lines and the bridges and so forth, where possible.

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:17.130
For instance, we have a blues that...

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:20.830
Well, you know, the 12-bar blues format we still utilize.

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:25.370
But where the blues goes up a fourth, ordinarily,

00:16:25.730 --> 00:16:27.570
we have a tune that doesn't go up a fourth.

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:30.000
it just stays on the same court and then

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.800
it resolves the final four bars such as any other minor blues would and we have

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:40.030
added a bridge to it would you think that oh a trumpet player from the early

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:45.470
days of jazz would be at home and the in the blues that you play now only I

00:16:45.470 --> 00:16:51.470
would say not so only because our idea of improv improvisation using modern

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:57.540
chords is as a little different but he could be reasonably at home if told in

00:16:57.540 --> 00:17:00.000
advance what to play well now how would you

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.780
how would you explain the difference between your idea of improvisation and his well i don't think

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:12.070
it's too complicated except for today instead of playing the exact chords we play uh different

00:17:12.070 --> 00:17:18.790
chords based upon the original we may go a key above a key below half step above half step below

00:17:18.790 --> 00:17:24.110
and we do a lot of uh passing chords from change to change

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:30.000
and sometimes in playing these like the next tune we're about to play the improvised

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.720
has to play the same chord for eight bars and after a while you know that can

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:38.850
get monotonous so if you don't do something else with it then you see what

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:43.040
I'm getting uh-huh well you're going to play now some other illustrations of

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:48.530
tunes that you use that are in the blues idiom and to which you have added to

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:53.970
particular modern embellishments and structure in the solos what ones are you

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:58.060
going to play well this next one was written by of all people an old blues

00:17:58.060 --> 00:18:00.000
singer whose name is Eddie Cleanhead

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:07.100
Vincent. Oh, yeah. And I think that he would know as much about the blues as any of the younger guys

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:15.350
around today. But he wrote this tune the way that we play it, which is there is no change up or

00:18:15.350 --> 00:18:21.980
forth at the beginning of the second four bars, and he has a bridge in it that is a regular

00:18:21.980 --> 00:18:27.320
conventional type bridge, except for the last four bars go to another direction instead of the

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:30.000
conventional way that bridges go.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:33.660
name of this? This one's called Arriving Soon. Arriving Soon, and then you're going to play

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:37.400
another one? That's right. All right, you'll tell us about that. That's right. All right, thank you

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:41.640
very much, Julian. Thank you very much. We've been listening to Julian Cannonball-Adderley

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:46.020
discuss some of the aspects of the music that he plays today in the relationship of blues to

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:52.110
modern jazz, a way in which the musicians utilize the older forms and still find renewed inspiration

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:57.530
in them. And now we're going to listen to the Julian Cannonball-Adderley group play Eddie

00:18:57.530 --> 00:18:59.220
Vincent's tune arriving soon.

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:39.570
that was arriving soon. Now, as I said before, the first eight bars of that tune

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:46.540
didn't resolve as normal blues tunes do. This time, the first eight bars does

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:51.590
resolve, but instead of resolving conventionally, it starts off

00:27:51.590 --> 00:27:55.500
unconventionally. It starts off with the seventh chord. There's a seventh chord

00:27:55.500 --> 00:28:00.000
that goes all the way through, and we have a bridge in this tune, too.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.790
which is a regular conventional bridge but the seventh chord feeling is carried

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:09.590
on throughout the tune. It's written by our bass player Sam Jones and it's called

00:28:09.590 --> 00:28:11.400
"Unit Seven."

00:35:01.850 --> 00:35:06.650
Well, we've just been enjoying a fascinating and instructive lecture demonstration by Cannonball

00:35:06.650 --> 00:35:12.730
Adderley, and I think that Cannonball is the living personification of the articular jazz

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:16.910
musician. Of course, he has an advantage over some in that he has actually been a teacher

00:35:16.910 --> 00:35:23.370
in schools himself, and he shows this experience when he talks to us on this program as he does

00:35:23.370 --> 00:35:30.000
when he talks to the audiences at nightclubs and concerts. It was particularly instructive, it seems to me, for Cannonball to

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:35.560
discuss the blues today, because the blues is one of the main springs of jazz music,

00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:40.540
has always been, and undoubtedly always will be, and one of the things that people who

00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:46.440
come to modern jazz at first blush get confused by the way the blues sounds, because it doesn't

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:51.710
sound the same as it sounded in the old traditional jazz days, and I think that Cannonball has

00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:56.070
done an excellent job on this program in explaining to us some of the differences, and now back

00:35:56.070 --> 00:35:57.290
to Cannonball Adderley.
